  "response": "For most of the twentieth century, the cause of peptic ulcer disease seemed settled. The prevailing medical view held that peptic ulcers were caused by stress and excess stomach acid, a model supported by studies of gastric secretion and by the clinical impression that ulcers flared in anxious, overworked patients. Because that view located the disease in acid itself, histamine H2-receptor blockers such as cimetidine were a standard ulcer treatment before the bacterial discovery. These drugs suppressed secretion and healed lesions, yet ulcers frequently returned once therapy stopped. Overturning so entrenched a consensus required not a single observation but a cumulative sequence of microscopic, microbiological, experimental, and therapeutic evidence.\n\nThe first element came from the microscope. In 1979 J. Robin Warren observed curved bacteria in gastric biopsy specimens, whereas his own post, a direct consequence of that prevailing stress-and-acid view, was that of a gastric surgeon, whose daily work lay in resecting diseased stomachs rather than in reading slides of their lining. That sighting came two years before Barry Marshall began collaborating with Warren in 1981. The dominance of cimetidine therapy is evidence for why that partnership formed: a treatment that healed ulcers without preventing relapse left an open problem attractive to a young physician in search of a research project. Together the two men documented the organism in patients with gastritis and ulcers and, after repeated failures, succeeded in culturing it. The bacterium was originally named Campylobacter pyloridis. In other words, Campylobacter pyloridis was the first name given to the organism now called Helicobacter pylori, before genetic and structural differences justified placing it in a genus of its own.\n\nAssociation, however, does not establish causation, and early animal models of the infection were unsatisfactory. Marshall therefore conducted the experiment on himself. Although Barry Marshall drank the bacterial culture in 1990 as a healthy volunteer whose stomach lining had appeared normal at endoscopy, the published outcomes of that test are irreconcilable. The two outcomes cannot both be true, and one of them must hold: either Marshall's self-experiment caused him to develop gastritis, or it caused him no stomach inflammation. Only the first outcome would satisfy Koch's postulates for the inflammatory stage of the disease, and repeat endoscopy with biopsy was the means by which the question had to be decided.\n\nEven a persuasive self-experiment could not change clinical practice on its own authority. Although many gastroenterologists initially rejected the bacterial hypothesis of ulcers, regarding it as incompatible with decades of acid physiology, antibiotic eradication of the bacterium markedly reduced peptic ulcer recurrence rates. That reduction resulted from the 1994 NIH Consensus Development Conference's conclusion that ulcer patients infected with Helicobacter pylori should receive antimicrobial therapy. The conference's recommendation settled the dispute, converting a contested hypothesis into a standard of care and replacing indefinite acid suppression with a finite course of curative treatment.\n\nThe bacterial account did not abolish every other cause of ulceration, and contemporary diagnosis reflects that plurality. In evaluating the patient, a clinician must recognize that at least one of two mechanisms is at work, perhaps both: the patient's peptic ulcer involves Helicobacter pylori infection, or it involves the use of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Testing for the organism and taking a careful medication history therefore precede any treatment decision, since eradication therapy addresses the first mechanism and withdrawal of the offending drug addresses the second. The revised understanding thus retains acid as the immediate agent of mucosal injury while identifying infection and drug exposure as the conditions under which that acid is able to damage the stomach and duodenum.",
